Files copied unmodified (byte-identical to the upstream files at the commit above; verified with cmp):
  LICENSE
  follower.urdf        simulation/follower.urdf (joints joint_1 .. joint_6, link-frame visuals with identity origins, placeholder limits)
  base_link.STL        printed base (Follower_Base)
  link_1.STL           printed shoulder rotation bracket (Follower_Shoulder_Rotation)
  link_2.STL           printed shoulder-to-elbow arm (Follower_Shoulder_To_Elbow)
  link_3.STL           printed elbow-to-wrist extension (Follower_Elbow_To_Wrist_Extension)
  link_4.STL           printed elbow-to-wrist link (Follower_Elbow_To_Wrist)
  link_5.STL           printed gripper static part (Follower_Gripper_Static_Part)
  link_6.STL           printed gripper moving part (Follower_Gripper_Moving_Part)
  base_link_motor.STL, link_1_motor.STL, link_2_motor.STL, link_3_motor.STL, link_4_motor.STL, link_5_motor.STL
                       the servos (and their screws, idler frames, hubs) of each link, one merged mesh per link; the source of the
                       two servo bodies below and read by tools/koch_ports.py. Not referenced by any part file. Not manifold
                       (touching shells): MuJoCo rejects them with inertia = exact, use them as visuals only.

Files derived (modified), written by `python tools/koch_ports.py --write-servo-meshes` (numpy + scipy ConvexHull / qhull; scipy 1.18 from a
on the laptop; the hull is deterministic for the same input):
  xl430_w250_t.stl     Dynamixel XL430-W250-T body, 192 triangles (convex hull of the body cut out of base_link_motor.STL: its largest
                       connected component, 3897 triangles). Servo frame: origin on the horn axis at the outer face of the horn hub,
                       z = outward horn normal, x along the long body axis towards the body centre. bbox x [-0.01125, 0.03525]
                       y [-0.01425, 0.01425] z [-0.0379, 0] m (46.5 x 28.5 x 37.9 mm; the 3.9 mm above the 34 mm datasheet height is the hub).
  xl330_m288_t.stl     Dynamixel XL330-M288-T body, 136 triangles (convex hull of the body + cover shell, two connected components, cut
                       out of link_2_motor.STL), same servo frame convention. bbox x [-0.0095, 0.0245] y [-0.01, 0.01] z [-0.0259, -0.0029] m
                       (34 x 20 x 23 mm; the output hub plane is 2.9 mm above the body face and is not part of the mesh).
  Modifications: (1) triangles of one servo cut out of the merged *_motor mesh by connected component (vertices merged at 0.1 um);
  (2) coordinates transformed from the link frame to the servo frame (rigid, metres); (3) replaced by the convex hull, because the cut-out
  bodies are not manifold (133 / 50 unmatched or non-manifold edges: MuJoCo 'faces of mesh have inconsistent orientation' with inertia =
  exact); the hull keeps the bounding box exactly. Both hulls load in MuJoCo with inertia exact and convex (checked on hfbox, mujoco wheel).
  The four XL330 bodies and the two XL430 bodies agree in the servo frame to 0.1 mm (bbox) / 0.42 mm (centroid, joint 5 and 6 servos
  sit 0.1 mm further from the horn plane of their joint origin: 3.0 mm vs 2.9 mm hub gap); the two XL430 shells differ by 3 % in volume (internal detail).

Not copied: leader-arm parts (hardware/leader), the gripper/camera collision meshes of the Menagerie / gym-lowcostrobot variants, the
STEP / SolidWorks files, the electrical wiring diagram. Joint limits are NOT taken from follower.urdf (placeholder -3.14 .. 3.14, effort 0.5):
the part files use the 'approximate joint limits' of MuJoCo Menagerie low_cost_robot_arm (f054586, Apache-2.0, not copied), see docs/research/koch-v1.1.md.
